-1 |text seldom seen since theatrical release 1970 myra breckinridge has become byword for cinematic debacles legendary proportions now last dvd unexpectedly handsome package unlikely win wide audiences today was when first released gore vidal 1968 bestseller was darkly satirical statement most filmmakers felt that the novel story structure and overall tone would not translate film and industry insiders were surprised when 20th century fox not only acquired the rights but also hired vidal adapt his novel the screen but studio executives soon had cold feet vidal adaptations were repeatedly rejected and novice writer director michael sarne was brought bring the film the screen studio executives hoped that sarne would tap into the youth market they saw target for the film but sarne proved even more out synch with the material than the executives themselves rewrite upon rewrite followed the cast sensing disaster became increasingly combative her dvd commentary star raquel welch says that she seldom had any idea what myra motives were from scene scene even within any single scene itself and that each person involved seemed making entirely different film the accompanying back story documentary rex reed says that myra breckinridge was film made bunch people who hid their dressing rooms while waiting for their lawyers return their calls the accuracy these comments are demonstrated the film itself the basics vidal story are there but not only has the story been shorn all broader implications seems have point and itself everything runs off multiple directions nothing connects and numerous scenes undercut whatever logic previous scenes might have had and while director sarne repeatedly states his commentary that wanted make the film pure farce the only laughs generated are accidental chief among these accidents mae west true that west unexpectedly well preserved appearance and that she had lost none her way with one liner but there getting around the fact that she her seventies and her conviction that she the still the sexiest trick shoe leather extremely unsettling say the least but worse really the fact that west outside her era her efforts translate herself into hip and happening persona results one the most embarrassing self caricatures ever seen film the remaining cast largely wasted raquel welch significantly underestimated actress plays the title role myra very much like barbie doll steroids non actor rex reed unexpectedly effective the role myron but the entire role essentially without point only john huston and cameo players john carradine jim backus william hopper and andy devine emerge relatively unscathed yes really the debacle everyone involved the film feared would fast when should slow slow when should fast relentlessly unfunny from start finish true that director sarne does have the occasional inspired idea his use film clips everyone from shirley temple judy garland create counterpoint the action but and large whenever sarne was presented with choice how something seems have made the wrong one the how and why that made clear sarne audio commentary sarne did not like the novel for that matter the subject matter general did not want write the screenplay but needed the money emphatically did not want direct the film but need the money makes very clear that disliked author gore vidal and rex reed one point flatly states that reed not nice person and this day considers that vidal and reed worked tandem sabotage the film because refused play into their homosexual agenda which when you come right down seems have been their desire that sarne actually film vidal novel rather than his own weirdly imagined take off although spends fair amount commentary time stating that the film widely liked the gay community sarne never quite seems understand that the appeal the film for gay audience arises from his ridiculously inaccurate depiction homosexual people when taken tandem with the film itself sarne emerges more than little homophobic and quite frankly the single worst choice writers and directors that could have been made for this project addition the sarne and welch commentaries and the making documentary the dvd release includes several trailers and two versions the film theatrical release version and restored version the only difference between the two that the final scene the restored version has been printed black and white the edits made before the film went into general release have not been restored but the documentary details what they were the widescreen transfers both are remarkably good and the sound quite fine but end where began this indeed film that will most interest film historians movie buffs and cult movie fans give three out five stars for their sake alone but everyone else should pass gary taylor aka gft amazon reviewer
