1 |text the ring was made from the only screenplay hitchcock wrote himself and deals many his earliest pictures with love triangle first glance looks like more cynical update the infidelity themed morality comedies cecil mille but more than that the first really competent hitchcock picture even was not yet using the ideas and motifs suspenseful thrillers was least developing the tools with which create suspense well being student the german expressionist style the rhythmic editing style sergei eisenstein had had its impact upon hitchcock but here keeps tempo not just with the edits but with the content the imagery this apparent from the opening shots where spinning fairground rides brilliantly establish smooth tempo and like eisenstein the editing style seems suggest sound xc2 x96 for example when split second shot the bell being rung flashed almost subconsciously hear the sound because the image jarring there also contrast particularly with silent films from the that the ring not cluttered with too many title cards much possible conveyed imagery and hitch has enough faith the audience either lip read least infer the meaning the bulk the characters speech and not done contrived symbolism overacting all done getting the right angles and the right timing particularly with point view shots well some strong yet subtle performances there are unfortunately few too many obvious expressionist devices particularly double exposures many which were unnecessary but there far less this than there the lodger let make few honourable mentions for the aforementioned actors first the stunningly handsome and very talented carl brisson the lead role spite his talent was first bit confused why got the role honest looks more like ballet dancer than pugilist but that just goes show how much know turns out brisson was fact former professional boxer and inexperienced acting playing his rival the competent ian hunter who would have lengthy career supporting roles right the 60s the most demanding role the ring has that given lillian hall davis torn between two lovers she pulls off very well however with emotive understated performance and shame her career never lasted the sound era and last but not least the great gordon harker provides some comic relief what probably his best ever role the ring climactic fight scene among the most impressive moments silent era hitchcock martin scorcese may have had his eye the ring when directed the fight scenes raging bull his watchword for these scenes was stay inside the ring the fight the ring starts off with some fairly regular long shots but when the action intensifies hitchcock drops right the middle with close ups and point view shots hitchcock aim always seems have been involve his audience and this was crucial his later career where the secret his success was often immersing the viewer the character fear paranoia the ring really deserves more recognition than the inferior but better known the lodger much more polished and professional work than the earlier picture and probably the best all his silent features
