1 |text edward burtynsky canadian photographer who makes art out the least artful objects imaginable everyday items such crates boxes metal containers etc items which most perceive utilitarian best and dismiss being utterly without aesthetic merit are instead converted into glorious objects art burtynsky camera achieves this result focusing the recurring colors and geometric patterns that are apparently ever present the industrialized world for those perceptive enough spot them that even heaps compacted trash can become objects beauty when seen through burtynsky lens but didn already know that from wall particularly interested photographing areas like mines and shipyards where man has already made incursions into nature which may explain why times even the people his pictures the workers those places with their uniform clothing and robotic movements become part the industrial landscape manufactured landscapes documentary about burtynsky work has much the feel koyaanisqatsi about dazzles with its richly variegated kaleidoscope images and patterns indeed director jennifer baichwal and cinematographer peter mettler capture the essence the original photos purely cinematic terms their own camera records burtynsky and his assistant running photo shoots factory china dockyard bangladesh and the construction site the massive three rivers gorge dam project china with their fluid camera work the filmmakers match point for point the beauty burtynsky images fact the movie opens with stunning eight minute long tracking shot chinese factory which hundreds similarly dressed workers toil away perfectly symmetrical and color coordinated rows the movie does less well when burtynsky gets around articulating the themes his work which quite frankly come out sounding confused contradictory and decidedly half baked best but purely aesthetic experience highlighting image and form that manufactured landscapes resonates most the case burtynsky perhaps picture really worth thousand words
