1 |text one the best documentaries released recent years some points hugo chavez was elected venezuela president 1998 his support largely coming from the poorer regions venezuela 2002 coup briefly deposed chavez the time irish filmmakers kim bartley and donnacha briain were caracas shooting documentary about chavez for british television their film deconstructs the coup and its aftermath and electrifyingly records history unfolding the spot outside and inside the presidential palace chavez aimed free venezuela from the free market policies imposed the though venezuela oil was already state owned was run for private benefit executives who chavez wished replace despite being the world fourth largest oil supplier venezuela remains swamped poverty its resources literally sucked away foreign multinational corporations the documentary begins portraying chavez first years president before the coup focuses his popularity with the poor and his various policies which proved popular with working class locals educational plans distribution the oil revenue grass root democracy etc chavez was huge proponent education and printed thousands copies the venezuelan constitution encouraging children and adults study and understand when chavez came power immediately pledged redistribute oil profits this understandably made the oil companies nervous media war broke out the six private stations promptly began opposing the state run station they questioned chavez motives sanity and sexual orientation without media support the coup would not have been successful the film makes clear that coups rely heavily the media disseminate information and that news can easily fabricated under the guise establishing democracy the opposition silenced the state run station dissolved the national electoral board supreme court national assembly and took control the military moneyed interests backed the military elite encouraged the and cia organised citizens march the presidential palace effect the coup snipers shot xc3 xa1vez supporters but the private media stations edited footage appeared that return fire was aimed the opposition march that fact had been safely diverted police went shooting rampage against chavez supporters further bloodying the streets chavez held captive refused resign course the media government then lied saying had resigned but chavez cabinet members communicated the truth the international community which eventually got the message back venezuela cable the people rose pressuring the return the president they had elected whom only referendum could constitutionally replace little over hour long this doc far too short nevertheless its engrossing piece journalism and deals with form media warfare which rarely gets touched upon makes great companion piece the battle algiers worth one viewing
