-1 |text dreamgirls despite its fistful tony wins incredibly weak year broadway has never been what one would call jewel the crown stage musicals however that not say that the right cinematic hands could not fleshed out and polished into something worthwhile screen unfortunately what transfers the screen basically slavishly faithful version the stage hit with all its inherent weaknesses intact first the score has never been one the strong points this production and the film does not change that factor there are lots songs perhaps too many but few them are especially memorable the closest any come catchy tunes are the title song and one night only the much acclaimed and telling you that not going less great song than dramatic set piece for the character effie jennifer hudson the film slick and technically well produced but the story and characters are surprisingly thin and lacking any resonance there some interest the opening moments watching jamie foxx svengali like manager manipulate his acts the top but that takes back seat the latter portion the film when the story conveniently tries cast him villain despite his having been right from business stand point for good majority the film beyonce knowles lovely and sings her songs perfectly well but stuck with character who basically all surface glitz anika noni rose the third member the dreamgirls trio literally has nothing for the entire film eddie murphy acquits himself well singer obviously based james brown but the role not especially meaty and ultimately has little impact foxx would seem ideal casting but seems oddly withdrawn and bored the film biggest selling point surely former american idol contestant oscar winner jennifer hudson the central role effie white the temperamental singer who gets booted from the group and makes triumphant closing act return for effie has always been big problem both the show and the movie the film obviously wants you feel sorry for her and rather ham handedly takes her side but hav