1 |text feroz abbas khan gandhi father film that sheds light the fractured relationship between the mahatma and his son harilal gandhi for story that dramatic the one this film attempts tell pity the director fails tell dramatically gandhi father narrated you like that boring history lesson that put you sleep school now the film aims convey one very interesting point the fact that gandhi his attempt fair person ended being unfair father this point made the film many times over and one the examples given make this point that scholarship england which gandhi twice denies his son instead showing how exactly harilal dealt with this betrayal and what went his head the director just moves along with the story thus never letting witness the growing resentment harilal feels towards his father which why when finally see outburst from harilal comes off looking like over reacting the point trying make here that never really get understand exactly why harilal became the rebel that did never really understand why turned islam and then back again hinduism the thing never really understand harilal all and that because the director this film too busy focusing mohandas karamchand gandhi and his role the freedom struggle story most are already familiar with put simply gandhi father promises examine the strained father son relationship but doesn much show where the cracks this relationship first set understand harilal had live with the burden being gandhi son but show why that was burden begin with show incidents their early conflict for example not enough that gandhi merely says opposed harilal early marriage tell why this opposition not enough that kasturba blames her husband for the way her son turned out for constantly shuttling him between schools gujarat and south africa for making him relocate every time gandhi needed relocate words are not enough show how these incidents shaped the character harilal gandhi what more instead sticking with the prickly theme this tenuous gandhi versus gandhi relationship the film goes off too many tangents thus diluting the impact the central theme this was never meant film about the struggle for independence and yet many occasions that exactly what seems like because the director feels almost obligated take through all the main events leading upto that historic moment even though much has relevance the film basic premise the stormy father son relationship you see the problem with this film not that bad film but certainly very confused film what happens harilal children after his wife death does ever have relationship with them where they suddenly vanish after that one scene which see them with the mahatma and kasturba none these questions are answered film that basically meant about relationships the gandhi family the film version immensely popular play directed feroz abbas khan himself gandhi father disappointment questions asked cinematically struggles translate the filmmaker ambitious intention the screen practically every single scene the film opens and closes with fade ins and fade outs never quite seamlessly leading into each other the positive side there inherent nobility the film which you recognise the filmmaker makes every effort deliver balanced narrative trying hard not take sides never once judging either father son painting neither the villain what the film does however make clear the fact that gandhi was difficult patriarch whose ideals may have shaped the nation but evidently alienated his family all the actors the film only akshaye khanna who really shines the role the luck deprived harilal gandhi wonderful performance and not easy since the role covers virtually the entire lifespan the character but akshaye brings rare concoction innocence and despondency that part and succeeds making harilal pitiable figure just watch him that scene which discovers his wife dead and you realise how much conveys through body language alone darshan jariwala meanwhile who plays gandhi senior adopts caricaturish approach playing the mahatma his later years but the way humanises the man his early years barrister south africa that the actor best contribution that role the abundantly gifted shefali shah plays kasturba the woman who meant torn this father son conflict but she unable bring across that feeling helplessness then really not much her fault the fault rickety script much effort gone into the making this film and that evident throughout but the film suffers from that inevitable flaw that eventually what you remember about when you leave the cinema just boring director feroz abbas khan gandhi father sincere effort yes but also film that could have done with much tighter screenplay what learn from the film that gandhi and harilal made each other very unhappy and with this film the director makes too
