1 |text there doubt that during the decade the 30s the names boris karloff and bela lugosi became sure guarantee excellent performances high quality horror films after being universal first monster the seminal classic dracula bela lugosi became the quintessential horror villain thanks his elegant style and his foreign accent sadly this last factor would also led him type casted during the 40s the same way boris karloff performance james whale frankenstein transformed him into the man look for when one wanted good monster course was only natural for these icons end sharing the screen and the movie that united them was 1934 the black cat this formula would repeated several films through the decade and director lambert hillyer mix horror and science fiction the invisible ray another those minor classics they did those years the invisible ray janos rukh boris karloff brilliant scientist who has invented device able show scenes our planet past captured rays light coming from the galaxy andromeda while showing his invention his colleagues felix benet bela lugosi and sir francis stevens walter kingsford they discover that thousands years ago meteor hit what now nigeria after this marvelous discovery rukh decides join his colleagues expedition africa looking for the landing place the mysterious meteor this expedition won any beneficial for rukh during the expedition his wife diane frances drake will fall love with ronald drake frank lawton expert hunter brought the stevens aid them their expedition however rukh will lose more than his wife that trip forever changed after being exposed the invisible ray the meteor written john colton who previously did the script for werewolf london the invisible ray had its roots original sci story howard higgin and douglas hodges given that this was movie with karloff and lugosi colton puts lot emphasis the horror side his story playing very effective way with the mad scientist archetype and adding good dose melodrama spice things one element that makes the invisible ray stand out among other horror films that era the way that colton plays with morality through the story that there aren exactly heroes and villains the classic style but people who make decisions and later face the consequences those choices many ways the invisible ray modern tragedy about obsessions guilt and revenge seasoned director low budget movies filmmaker lambert hillyer got the chance make films for universal pictures when the legendary studio was facing serious financial troubles thanks his experience working with limited resources hillyer films were always very good looking despite the budgetary constrains and the invisible ray was not exception while nowhere near the stylish gothic atmosphere previous universal horror films hillyer movie effectively captures the essence colton script gives this movie dark and morbid mood more tone with pulp novels than with straightforward sci finally word must said about hillyer use special effects for extremely low budget film they look lot better than the ones several movies the era usual movie with lugosi and karloff the performances this legends are extraordinary quality the film protagonist boris karloff simply perfect his portrayal man blinded the devotion his work that fails see the evil unleashes his colleague benet bela luogis simply joy watch stealing every scene and showing what underrated actor was rukh wife frances drake extremely effective truly helping her character become more than damsel distress still two the movie highlights are the performances kemble cooper mother rukh and beulah bondi lady arabella the two actresses make the most their limited screen time making unforgettable their supporting roles frank lawton also good his role but nothing surprising when compared the rest the cast one judges this movie under today standards very easy dismiss another cheap science fiction film with bad special effects and carelessly jumbled pseudoscience however that would mistake despite its low budget remarkably well done for its time the top that considering that the movie was made when the nuclear era was about begin and radioactivity was still relatively new concept ideas about the dangers radioactivity are frighteningly accurate one final thing worthy point out the interesting way the script handles the relationships between characters specially the friendship and rivalry that exists between the obsessive rukh and the cold benet this allows great scenes between the two iconic actors while nowhere near the gothic expressionism the frankenstein movies nor the elegant suspense the black cat lambert hillyer the invisible ray definitely minor classic amongst universal pictures catalog horror films with one the most interesting screenplays 30s horror this mixture suspense horror and science fiction one severely underrated gem that even now delivers good dose entertainment courtesy two the most amazing actors the horror genre ever had boris karloff and bela lugosi
