zen and quakerly pretty radical for stoic clergyman but with all the beauty and contemplation and even though the macleans are truly god fearing scripture heeding household how that rev maclean family unraveling paul true perfection fishes the river but feeling the pull gambling and boozing while his family doesn know how keep him from winding where seems headed mom dad and brother all seem have the same quiet desperation not knowing what they should doing and why they can seem help pauly just waves all off with grin and his irresistible charm but the junior brother losing his grip norman starts getting his life track finding love and career but paul continues slide the family that loves him watches helplessly mother father brother flounder their own ways trying help but none very effectively how can family that loves each other much ill equipped handle this how can someone artful and full grace when out god nature yet somehow unfit unwilling fit into the constructs society that god peoples have made for themselves these are all questions norman will ponder his entire life the eternal words beneath the smooth stones the river forever haunt him yet keep their secrets the movie beautiful watch this certainly god country and filming won oscar director robert redford plays with the story from the book and teases the narration bit follow the emotional pattern presenting and works well but back and read the book too you see norman made connections with his old man even deeper than the movie can suggest and you see the places where the storyteller very words gurgle and sing right off the page with exuberance river running through leading into the unknown
