1 |text special day ettore scola italy canada 1977 every once while you come across film that really touches nerve this one offers very simple premise almost flawlessly executed every way and incredibly moving the same time surprising ettore scola una giornate particulare relatively unheralded even hated some critics time out calls rubbish and leonard maltin somewhat milder pleasant but trifling disagree not only because this film deeply moving but within its simple story shows more insights about daily life fascist italy than most films seen the cinematography distinctly unflashy even bit bland and the storyline straightforward which might explain the film relative unpopularity considering late 70s audiences weren exactly spoiled with great italian films even stranger this one didn really catch with the critics the film begins with ten minute collage archive footage from hitler visit italy may 8th 1938 set against this background first meet antonietta loren lonely love ridden housewife with six children roman apartment building one day when her beo escapes she meets her neighbour gabriele mastroianni who seems only one the building not attending the ceremonies well mannered cultured and soon she attracted him during the whole film hear the fascist rally from the radio the concierge hollering through the courtyard scola playfully uses the camera make part the proceedings after the opening scene the camera swanks across the courtyard the modernist hypermodern the time apartment block seemingly searching for our main characters whom haven met yet marcello mastrionani and sophia loren are unforgettable the two leading roles all the more astonishing since they are cast completely against type canadian born john vernon plays loren husband but only screen the first and last scene figure his voice must have been dubbed since not italian descent and never lived there knowledge cannot imagine speaks italian his voice has been dubbed didn notice all the contrary completely believable italian even more than the rest the cast the story simple but extremely effective the performances are outstanding the ending just perfect and the framing doesn come off overly pretentious but works completely don miss out this one camera obscura
