ioning pale look and those minor mannerisms just seem haunt you she spirit you don want cross paths with yet alone let her see you accomplished performance marvelously moody adrian rawlins the solicitor arthur kidd does hold all together support are solid turns bernard hepton david daker clare holman and david ryall drawing heavy its lushly sombre rural town and foggy coastal locations adds more the realistically eerie plight and the centre piece were everything unfolds the forlorn time worn victorian house that comprehensively suffocates the air with constant fear director herbert wise carefully fabricates alarming imagery that slowly covers one secretive piece time smoothly paved out rhythm well judged contriving instead going out shock some scenes contain distressing intensity that won let the sound effects are masterfully used surrounding and disorienting the air rachel portman harrowing musical score knows how get under your skin during those eerie moments and then stay with you this rarity made for television feat stimulating rich and unsettling supernatural spook fest might not share much new the sub genre but competently sticks strengths deliver what counts this curse
