what fudged not made clear enough that esther actually love with woodcourt when she agrees marry john jarndyce neither clear that they have agreed not announce their engagement why ada and rick secret marriage omitted one episode they are merely lovers the next people are suddenly referring them husband and wife mrs rouncewell only introduced late stage the story and sargeant george estrangement from his family left unexplained the means which she discovered tulkinhorn dedication maintaining the honour and respectability the dedlock family understated his motive for persecuting lady dedlock more obscure than need the involvement the brick makers with both tom and later lady dedlock somewhat opaque not obvious that guppy renews his offer esther because her smallpox scars have all but vanished this only selection there are others they are not major problems and the main thrust the story clear enough nonetheless they are minor irritations that detract from its power you shouldn have puzzle over little plot points however there are more important structural problems that weaken the story its later stages the whole business tulkinhorn murder somewhat thrown away bucket immediately pinpoints hortense suspect which undermines the suspense sergeant george predicament and the importance finding mrs rouncewell also diminishes the impact the sub plot which suspicion thrown lady dedlock and weakens the scene which hortense unmasked front sir lester more serious problem that the murder its investigation and the subsequent search for lady dedlock dominate the story for over hour during which time completely lose sight the other main plot strand the legal case and its effect rick his failing finances his gouging vholes and skimpole ada despair his declining health and are all put hold for entire episode this may how dickens wrote the book haven read for years but good screenplay should keep the different plot strands moving forward together finally smallweed role the story diminished that almost superfluous his discovery the new will that triggers the final phase the story also thrown away happens off screen despite all this still very good production many the performances are outstanding individual scenes are beautifully realised its accumulating sense tragedy very powerful would still recommending superb adaptation great book had not been for the 2005 production fact probably wouldn fully aware its defects hadn seen how andrew davies did better have been critical davies jane austen adaptations but have admit that really knows how tame dickens sprawling books this impressive and gripping drama and well worth seven hours anybody time nonetheless its probable fate viewed mainly cross reference the near definitive 2005 version
