1 |text after his classic film noir homage chinatown roman polanski returned the themes that had given him his greatest hits the 60s with this creepy psychological horror which like repulsion and rosemary baby deals with the paranoia and claustrophobia generated apartment living claustrophobic environments are the ones which polanski best creating and this has the most suffocating and confined picture ever created the emphasis side walls and distant vanishing points greater than ever and even the small number exterior scenes the sky rarely glimpsed but the tenant not just confined spatially but also the intensity with which focuses its protagonist trelkovsky played polanski himself not only every scene virtually every shot when not screen more often than not the camera becomes trelkovsky point view and course almost everywhere looks sees his own reflection staring back him mirror can think any film that more about the internalisation and solitude one character some psychological thrillers like peeping tom manipulate into feeling sorry for the mentally ill protagonist others like psycho attempt depth scientific analysis his mental condition the tenant fits into neither these categories xc2 x96 simply immerses completely inside trelkovsky experience without demanding actually understand appreciate what going inside his head feel his paranoia and obsession even though constantly revealed that they are irrational polanski was also master the slowly unfolding horror film often his horrors there ambiguity whether there actually anything sinister going but they are among the most effective frightening audiences why precisely because they unfold slowly and invest much time painstakingly setting situations that they immerse the viewer paranoia much later polanski horror the ninth gate bit mess plot wise but least still manages achieve that creeping sense dread this rare chance see polanski himself major role his talent front the camera was good behind and absolutely perfect 