1 |text esther kahn young jewish woman living overcrowded jewish ghetto 19th century england she surrounded looming oppressive dreary featureless worn brick architecture narrow sidewalks and streets blacked out windows and hordes black and brown jacketed crowds she lives tiny apartment with her large family whom operate clothes shop within the apartment child she worked had privacy wore colourless clothing shared bed and remained silent avert the mockery her mother and siblings who ridiculed her for mimicking them out boredom young woman her life remains the same she has privacy lives state mental and physical hebetude and lethargy and inertia exudes blank featureless expression clothed plain unremarkable clothing and continuously oppressed and dwarfed the grey mundane massively imposing buildings and narrow streets and narrow hallways and narrow doorways and her loud mouthed mother and siblings and the prosaic banal lifestyle her family her only form mental escape the yiddish theatre sitting the balcony front row leaning over the rail there vast space between her mind and the stage space that enables her breathe think feel and yearn yet despite the freedom thought the open stage provides for esther her face and body remain torpidly somnolent impassive dispassionate the plain and common looking summer phoenix brilliantly conveys esther emotionless demeanour summer esther does not convey any desire want anything anticipate anything after unusual explosive confrontation with her mother esther finally decides break free from the bleak life she trapped she eventually cast minor parts few stage plays and meets nathan quellen portrayed quintessential british actor ian holm who commences teach esther the technical skill acting from this point forward esther begins grueling dual journey learning how act and learning how feel she begins experiencing emotions she never felt before and she begins gaining the experience she needs fully comprehend and wield the technical aspects acting nathan walks her across the stage through the physical and emotional steps surprise hesitancy anger disgust self loathing etc she then begins walking through those emotions her personal life there are three truths nathan tells her the truth how character reacts the truth how the actor would react and the truth that character and actor are not the same person these technical steps and three truths slowly deconstruct esther defenses and lead her two edifying experiences the denouement the film which mark the beginning her freedom thought movement and emotion esther kahn technically challenging film watch because its odd and narrow camera shots lackluster photo direction which conveys the realistic lackluster setting the ghetto and summer phoenix characterless and insipid and unappealing portrayal which brilliantly conveys kahn mental and physical hebetude and lethargy and lackluster nature must see film for people who want learn the technical craft acting and for people who appreciate minimalistic films and character studies
