1 |text former cambridge contemporary simon heffer today writer and journalist has put forward the theory that just british film makers the eighties were often critical what they called thatcher britain the ealing comedies were intended satires attlee britain the britain which had come into being after the labour victory the 1945 general election this theory was presumably not intended apply say kind hearts and coronets which anything satire the edwardian upper classes the ladykillers the lavender hill mob both which may contain some satire but are not political nature can however applied most the other films the series especially passport pimlico pimlico least was the forties predominantly working class district london set the north bank the thames about mile from victoria station not quite correct say has often been said that the film about pimlico declaring itself independent britain what happens that ancient charter comes light proving that the fifteenth century the area was ceded king edward the duchy burgundy this means that technically pimlico independent state and has been for nearly five hundred years irrespective the wishes its inhabitants the government promise pass special act parliament rectify the anomaly but until the act receives the royal assent the area remains outside the united kingdom and british laws not apply because pimlico not subject british law the landlord the local pub free open whatever hours chooses and local shopkeepers can sell whatever they please whomever they please unhindered the rationing laws when other traders start moving into the area sell their goods the streets the british authorities are horrified what they regard legalised black marketeering and seal off the area try and force the burgundians the people pimlico have renamed themselves surrender many the ealing comedies have their central theme the idea the little man taking the system either individual happens the man the white suit the lavender hill mob part larger community happens whisky galore the titfield thunderbolt the central theme passport that ordinary men and women taking bureaucracy and government imposed regulations which seemed increasingly important feature life the britain the forties the film particular target the rationing system during the war the system had been accepted most people necessary sacrifice the fight against nazism but became increasingly politically controversial when the government tried retain peacetime was major factor the growing unpopularity the attlee administration which had been elected with large majority 1945 and organisations such the british housewives league were set campaign for the abolition rationing cannot agree with the reviewer who stated that the main targets the film satire were the spivs black marketeers who play relatively minor part the action the housewives league who not appear all the satire very much targeted the bureaucrats who are portrayed either having rules for rules sake mentality desire pass the buck and avoid having take any action all suspect that the film were made today would have different ending with pimlico remaining independent british version monaco san marino indeed suspect that today this concept would probably serve the basis sitcom rather than film 1949 however four years after the end the war the film makers were keen stress patriotism and british identity the film ends with pimlico being reabsorbed into britain one the best known lines from the film always were english and always will english and just because are english that sticking for our right burgundians there sharp contrast between the rather heartless attitude officialdom with the common sense tolerance and good humour the cockneys pimlico all which are presented being quintessentially british characteristics most the action takes place during summer drought and sweltering heatwave but the last scene after pimlico has rejoined the the temperature drops and starts pour with rain global warming may have altered things slightly but for many years part being british was the ability hold the belief whatever statistics might say the contrary that britain had abnormally wet climate the ability make jokes about that climate was equally important there good performance from stanley holloway arthur pemberton the grocer and small time local politician who becomes the prime minister free pimlico and amusing cameo from margaret rutherford batty history professor the main however this appropriately enough for film about small community pulling together example ensemble acting with real star performances but with everyone making contribution excellent film lacks the ill will and rancour many more recent satirical films but its wit and satire are less effective for all that remains one the funniest satires bureaucracy ever made and with the possible exception kind hearts and coronets personal favourite among the ealing comedies
