1 |text flynn known mostly for his swashbuckling roles and his bedroom antics takes different tack with this film and works beautifully playing real life boxing champ jim corbett flynn turns the charm full blast makes his way from stifled san francisco bank teller celebrated pugilist all the while setting one eye society deb smith and best pal carson attend illegal bare knuckle fight and are arrested along with scores other men and dog including prominent judge the next day gets chance via smith gain entrance the judge private club uses this opportunity weasel his way into the good graces its exclusive members and land spot the club resident boxer his unusually adept skill the sport soon has him taking all comers and including the world champion john sullivan bond flynn downright magical here the epitome charm charisma and appeal this role looks terrific especially hangover scene with his hair mussed and wearing white union suit and does virtually all his own stuntwork impressively his line delivery delicious and credible and sympathetic and the same time duplicitous and rascally smith exudes class and taste from every pore and good match for flynn this stage needed female costar who could stand his advances and reputation was undergoing statutory rape charges the time and she does admirably she repulsed his freshness and cavalier attitude yet can hardly help but fall under his enchanting spell bond incredibly burly brawny and towering yet tender when the script calls for amusing support provided young and ebullient carson frawley his dependably cantankerous self flynn manager the rest the cast excellent well including flynn rambunctious family and assortment stuffy nob hill types the whole thing beautifully appointed and securely directed few the sets are amazingly presented some smith gowns border the garish but she suits the upswept hairstyles very well terrific glimpse into the earliest days championship boxing but also much more some like the character traits shown flynn enhanced exaggerated for entertainment purposes but lot authentic like the methods and costumes shown the fight scenes one line particularly memorable believe you like more than like you but entirely possible that love you more than you love classic romantic dialogue and there are more than few zingers sprinkled throughout the script well
